<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02358</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d40e693e-713c-468e-be9a-e21818245efe</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>For questions 1 - 5, the standard form of an equation is \(ax^2+bx+c\). For each expression, find the missing constant \(c\) that would make the expression a perfect square.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="74779890-74be-4c6e-abac-e9c6c8418736" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2−6x+ \underline{\;\;c\;\;}\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 9 can be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: 9 can be added to make the expression a perfect square. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="aff55de0-7356-4328-86a8-3cef24731e96" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+2x+ \underline{\;\;c\;\;}\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1 can be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: 1 can be added to make the expression a perfect square. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="3e35319d-0d6b-4438-b898-a83eb4302848" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+14x+ \underline{\;\;c\;\;}\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 49 can be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: 49 can be added to make the expression a perfect square. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="9dad8fdb-86ab-4fc5-91d4-2374565cfed6" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2−4x+ \underline{\;\;c\;\;}\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 4 can be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: 4 can be added to make the expression a perfect square. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="76a7bb49-df6b-4f07-9e91-fa1907096032" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+24x+ \underline{\;\;c\;\;}\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 144 can be added to make the expression a perfect square.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: 144 can be added to make the expression a perfect square. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="1a8021f2-29b7-460c-a547-5ada95fee16f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>If \(x^2−6x+c\) is factored to be \((x \_\_\_\_\_)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression? Remember to include the sign.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! -3 </para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 9.2.2 and 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is -3.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="d27cd74b-c244-438d-95bf-926db14292d5" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>If \(x^2+2x+c\) is factored to be \((x \_\_\_\_\_)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression? Remember to include the sign.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! +1 </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.4.</para><para>The correct answer is +1.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="34d2148f-1960-4081-8dd9-f3562a759993" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>If \(x^2+14x+c\) is factored to be \((x \_\_\_\_\_)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression? Remember to enter the sign.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! +7</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.2.</para><para>The correct answer is +7.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="0caa8b15-aa41-4a47-895e-6465577e9e88" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>If \(x^2−4x+c\) is factored to be \((x \_\_\_\_\_)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression? Remember to enter the sign.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! -2 </para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.2.</para><para>The correct answer is -2.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c08b5393-28bf-4e98-96e0-02d1f5945809" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>If \(x^2+24x+c\) is factored to be \((x \_\_\_\_\_)^2\), what is the missing value of the factored form of this expression? Remember to enter the sign.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! +12</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.2.</para><para>The correct answer is +12.
      </para></solution></exercise></section><!--3a-->
<para>For each equation in questions 11 - 14, use the following scenario.</para>
<para><quote>Jada looks at Mai's work and is confused. She doesn't see how Mai got her answer.</quote><quote>Complete Mai's missing steps by answering the questions to help Jada see how Mai solved the equation.</quote></para><para>\(x^2+12x=13\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>\(x^2\;+\;12x\; +\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\) \(=\;13\;+\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> \((x+6)^2=49\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>\(\sqrt{(x+6)^2}=\sqrt{49}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> \(x+6= \pm\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 - </emphasis>\(x+6=\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 -</emphasis> \(x\;+\;6=\) \(-\) \(\_\_\_\_\_\)</para>
<para>\(x=1\) or \(x=-13\)</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="daff277b-a353-4044-96ee-ad9ea59081f8" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the missing term in Step 1?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 36</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 36.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="27ace37f-eb5a-468d-97a5-f15cc4b7470f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the missing term in Steps 4, 5, and 6?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 7</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 7.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c07dfbfc-4a8b-4a72-91ec-f847b6fad843" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value of \(x\) in Step 5?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! 1</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is 1.
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c88b63c7-9f5e-4680-8d0e-da4c7639548c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>What is the value of \(x\) in Step 6?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! -13</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is -13.
      </para></solution></exercise></section><para>For questions 15 - 20, solve choose the equivalent equation with a perfect square on one side.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="a68974c6-171a-48d6-8ba7-8ee9f75aedfa" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+8x=2\) </para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((x+4)^2=18\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="69d3ec36-966a-47c0-9312-eb11834874ef" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+10x=-13\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\) </para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((x+5)^2=12\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="e21c7ba8-89c4-44ee-815e-7ba383005675" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2−14x=5\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((x−7)^2=54\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="38c7644a-a7e8-4d2f-9bd0-dcf06b374b59" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+2x=0\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((x+1)^2=1\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="802db0f9-423e-49e8-a434-b7ddff12b747" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2+4x−5=0\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((x+2)^2=9\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((x+2)^2=9\).
      </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c20e168d-e932-42c0-a244-a690ebff838c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2−20x=-9\)</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\((x−7)^2=54\)</para><para>\((x+5)^2=12\)</para><para>\((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>\((x+4)^2=18\)</para><para>\((x+1)^2=1\)</para><para>\((x+2)^2=9\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \((x−10)^2=91\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \((x−10)^2=91\).
      </para></solution></exercise></section><!--5a-->
<para>For questions 21-23, solve each equation by completing the square, then choose the <emphasis>two</emphasis> roots.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise id="01ba74f0-4495-49ae-a6f3-b852b9226b0d" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2−6x+5=12\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-1\) and \(x=7\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-1\) and \(x=7\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="026ea025-d407-4e51-ad91-854415e91c82" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(x^2−2x=8\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-2\) and \(x=4\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-2\) and \(x=4\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="cc4974dc-f7ef-4f80-b674-84e87c241f3c" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>\(11=x^2+4x−1\)</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=-6\) and \(x=2\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answers are: \(x=-6\) and \(x=2\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b1e1f763-6ea6-46bd-8a02-8cd017f9bc9b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Solve the equation \(x^2−18x+60=-21\) by completing the square.</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x = -21\)</para><para>\(x = 9\)</para><para>\(x = 39\)</para><para>\(x = 78\)</para><para>\(x = -39\)</para></problem><solution><para>Correct! \(x=9\)</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 9.2.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: \(x=9\).
      </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
